WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.933
So my name is Erling Norrby, and I am trained to be a virologist,

00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:16.300
and I have taught about viruses at the medical school

00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:19.867
but also I have overriding administrative responsibilities

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:23.500
both at the Karolinska Institute, the school of medicine in Stockholm,

00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:27.033
and the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences in Stockholm.

00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:31.667
And the story I am going to tell you about is the

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:34.000
discovery of the structure of DNA.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:38.600
And to those of you in the audience who are a little younger would think

00:00:38.633 --> 00:00:42.167
but the structure of DNA... that has been known forever.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.333
But that is not so.

00:00:43.367 --> 00:00:49.200
So, there was a true discovery of the structure and as a consequence of that,

00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:52.167
one also started understanding the enormous impact

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:53.733
that this molecule had.

00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:58.667
Before this discovery, it was not known. In fact, there was considerable debate

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.333
on the relative role of proteins versus nucleic acids,

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:07.333
and the emphasis was on the proteins because the proteins

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:11.533
have much more diversity, 20 amino acids that could be varied indefinitely.

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:15.967
Whereas the DNA was a rather boring molecule with just four nucleotides

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.400
and there were also some incorrect data suggesting that the relative representation

00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:28.300
of the 4 bases was rather constant in different types/kinds of DNA.

00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:31.267
So how was this discovery made?

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:36.633
Well, what happened was that in the 1940s, the development

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:40.300
of the field of physics had been very, very powerful,

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:45.000
and physicists thought that they might have something to contribute to the field of biology.

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:53.367
So a lot of physicists trained to understand the major questions in biology at the time,

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.033
and they started to study biological phenomena.

00:01:57.067 --> 00:02:02.667
In England, the Medical Research Council decided to really invest in it

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:06.033
to stimulate physicists to study biological phenomena.

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:08.967
And there were two centers that were established.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.500
One was at the Cavendish Laboratory, Cambridge,

00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:15.300
and that was managed by Lawrence Bragg.

00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:20.000
He became the head of that institution in the early 1930s.

00:02:20.033 --> 00:02:26.300
And the other was at King's College in London.

00:02:26.333 --> 00:02:31.100
And the person who was in charge there was John Randall

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:36.333
who had during the war made... (He was a physicist)

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:42.033
made very important contributions to the development of the RADAR

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:46.767
that was very critical in deciding the outcome of the Second World War.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:53.033
But there was a kind of gentlemen's agreement between these two different laboratories,

00:02:53.067 --> 00:02:56.567
and also supported by the Medical Research Council,

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.267
namely that in Cambridge one would study protein structure,

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.200
and that King's College they wanted to study other structures,

00:03:03.233 --> 00:03:05.033
including nucleic acids.

00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:12.333
And the two leading figures at the Cavendish laboratory was Max Perutz and John Kendrew.

00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:21.500
They were crystallographers who really embarked on an almost impossible project

00:03:21.533 --> 00:03:26.967
namely to study very huge molecules with a complexity

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.333
that one predicted that it could not be done.

00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:34.267
But they started this in the early '30s,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.967
and then they developed this technique step by step,

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:46.600
way into the 1950s, and then later in the '50s came much better computers.

00:03:46.633 --> 00:03:49.633
And that should be emphasized that the possibility

00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:54.700
of processing huge amounts of information helped in this analysis.

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:59.767
And eventually one managed to define the three dimensional structure of very complex

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.333
protein structures like hemoglobin and myoglobin.

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:05.500
So that was the protein studies.

00:04:05.533 --> 00:04:12.700
But at the King's College, there was a person called Maurice Wilkins,

00:04:12.733 --> 00:04:18.033
who had joined Randall already before he came to King's College

00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:20.533
at St. Andrew's in Scotland.

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:23.800
And he was trying to study nucleic acids.

00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:30.700
In January 1951, he was joined by a female scientist by the name of Rosalind Franklin

00:04:30.733 --> 00:04:37.233
who came from France where she studies inorganic carbon compounds.

00:04:37.267 --> 00:04:41.633
She had very little knowledge about biology.

00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:47.167
And she was given this task to help or to study DNA,

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.733
and then she thought that the mission was to do it on her own responsibility.

00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:55.133
Wilkins thought that they should do it as a collaboration.

00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:58.767
But they never developed the collaboration between these two persons.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:06.533
And there's very special stories about how they each one of them,

00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:09.067
tried to approach this problem.

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:15.967
What really turned the whole thing was when in Autumn of 1950,

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.733
Jim Watson came to Cambridge.

00:05:19.767 --> 00:05:23.133
It's a long story of why he ended up there, because he was on scholarship in Denmark.

00:05:23.167 --> 00:05:26.867
but he was dissatisfied with that, and he had this idea

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:31.267
that he thought that DNA had something to do with the genetic material.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:33.033
And he was a little obsessed by that.

00:05:33.067 --> 00:05:38.167
And then he was in 1950, he was in Naples and heard

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:43.467
a lecture by Wilkins about the attempts to study the structure of DNA

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:51.200
by crystallographic analysis, and that further convinced him that this was the thing to do.

00:05:51.233 --> 00:05:55.133
But he was not satisfied with his time in Denmark,

00:05:55.167 --> 00:06:01.333
so with some help from his mentors, he could move to the Cavendish laboratories.

00:06:01.367 --> 00:06:04.067
And who did he meet there?

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:06.933
He met Francis Crick. Francis Crick was about 12 years older

00:06:06.967 --> 00:06:12.967
than what Jim Watson was, but the two of them really formed a remarkable couple,

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.600
and they stimulated each other.

00:06:14.633 --> 00:06:19.467
And even though the Cavendish laboratory was supposed to work on proteins,

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.033
then these two gentlemen couldn't refrain

00:06:22.067 --> 00:06:26.300
from discussing nucleic acid and its possible structure.

00:06:26.333 --> 00:06:28.700
And they even made a little model of that,

00:06:28.733 --> 00:06:34.100
that was then described to them to be completely wrong.

00:06:34.133 --> 00:06:38.200
They had a triple helix. They had bases extending into the periphery.

00:06:38.233 --> 00:06:43.167
And it was heavily criticized. Clearly it was wrong.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:48.833
And then Bragg decided that they were not allowed to work on nucleic acids.

00:06:48.867 --> 00:06:55.267
But one can see in the documents that there are that they kept on thinking about that.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:01.033
They met Chargaff, the famous person who had already at this time

00:07:01.067 --> 00:07:02.767
said that the amount of nucleotides,

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:10.100
A and T and G and C, that they are in equal proportion in each of these pairs.

00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:12.367
And no one had fully understood why this was important.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:19.733
So anyhow Watson and Crick, they were not allowed to work on the nucleic acid,

00:07:19.767 --> 00:07:26.200
but then things started to happen because Linus Pauling had had a fantastic advance.

00:07:26.233 --> 00:07:29.067
He was the giant in chemistry of his time.

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:32.633
He won the Nobel prize in '54 for his studies of the chemical bond.

00:07:32.667 --> 00:07:38.433
But all of that time he had a higher contribution in terms of the structure of proteins

00:07:38.467 --> 00:07:41.567
particularly the alpha- helix.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:45.667
And that was a little to the chagrin of the Cavendish group,

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.400
because they would have liked to be ahead of Pauling.

00:07:48.433 --> 00:07:51.967
And then Pauling decided no, I am going to go for the holy grail.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.367
I am going to study the structure of DNA.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:58.533
And the rumors started to spread,

00:07:58.567 --> 00:08:08.333
in '52 it was. And then Linus Pauling's son, Peter Pauling, was at the Cavendish laboratory.

00:08:08.367 --> 00:08:10.967
And so he received all the information from his father,

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:18.267
and in around December 1952 there was a manuscript from Pauling,

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:21.567
about the structure of DNA, and of course Watson and Crick

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:28.200
got all very, very concerned, because maybe Pauling had really solved the structure.

00:08:28.233 --> 00:08:32.267
They started to look at his manuscript, and to their big surprise,

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:37.867
they found that this giant in chemistry had made some major, major mistakes.

00:08:37.900 --> 00:08:40.533
In fact, the model he proposed was very similar

00:08:40.567 --> 00:08:43.333
to the model that Watson and Crick had proposed a year before.

00:08:43.367 --> 00:08:46.267
And had been demonstrated to them to be all wrong.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:55.100
This related to what Bragg said because scientists are competitive,

00:08:55.133 --> 00:09:00.367
and he wouldn't like to see Pauling coming ahead in his field.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.333
So he said to Watson and Crick, okay, you can work on DNA.

00:09:03.367 --> 00:09:07.600
That was about Christmas time in 1952.

00:09:07.633 --> 00:09:14.667
Then there were three facts that added to this very intense development

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.533
in January and February of 1953,

00:09:17.567 --> 00:09:19.767
and I can almost follow it from minute to minute.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.800
So what were these things?

00:09:21.833 --> 00:09:29.067
Well, Rosalind Franklin had clarified that there were two forms of DNA.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.200
And both of them could crystallize,

00:09:31.233 --> 00:09:35.433
but the B form was a little wetter, there was more water,

00:09:35.467 --> 00:09:40.133
It didn't crystallize as well, so she was mostly concerned with the A form.

00:09:40.167 --> 00:09:44.867
But what she didn't understand was that they were two forms of the same molecule.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:51.900
And if she had really decided to work on the B form,

00:09:51.933 --> 00:09:54.733
she might have made even better headway

00:09:54.767 --> 00:09:58.300
than what she did, but she did achieve very important data.

00:09:58.333 --> 00:10:05.000
Watson and Crick knew about this because Perutz,

00:10:05.033 --> 00:10:11.200
who was on the review committee to look at the data from King's College,

00:10:11.233 --> 00:10:16.032
leaked the information that was in the report from December 1952.

00:10:16.033 --> 00:10:20.200
[00:10:16.02 ]Now that has been very much discussed and Perutz himself has said that

00:10:20.233 --> 00:10:24.700
well, I was immature, and I didn't... I mean it wasn't secret material, so I shared it.

00:10:24.733 --> 00:10:31.733
But the other thing was something that happened, and that was in January,

00:10:31.767 --> 00:10:36.467
because then Watson was visiting King's College and then he was shown this picture.

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:41.500
And it had been taken by Rosalind Franklin and her collaborator Gosling,

00:10:41.533 --> 00:10:50.300
as named on this, and it's a very, very special picture, and when Watson saw this,

00:10:50.333 --> 00:10:54.067
and he received this information via Wilkins,

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:59.033
he immediately understood this must be a double helix.

00:10:59.067 --> 00:11:02.167
And of course Crick who was the one who really knew structure,

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:04.667
could really support that and conclude that it was

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:09.000
a double helix with the two helices running in the opposite direction.

00:11:09.033 --> 00:11:14.267
Here is the point in history where we are never going to know the truth.

00:11:14.300 --> 00:11:18.600
Because it says in many books that this picture was taken from Rosalind Franklin

00:11:18.633 --> 00:11:23.300
without her knowledge. And the family still believes that that is the case.

00:11:23.333 --> 00:11:25.233
But you have the other side of the version

00:11:25.267 --> 00:11:28.967
where it says in the annotated book of the Double Helix

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:35.600
where it says that Franklin gave this picture to Wilkins as a gift and he could use it.

00:11:35.633 --> 00:11:40.000
Personally I think the truth is somewhat in between these two.

00:11:40.033 --> 00:11:44.233
I don't think really that the photograph was really stolen from Franklin.

00:11:44.267 --> 00:11:47.767
If that had been the case, she would have been much more upset.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:50.700
And she was person of considerable temperament.

00:11:50.733 --> 00:11:55.100
And later on this picture 51 has been used to illustrate

00:11:55.133 --> 00:11:58.867
the structure of DNA, and used by Crick in reviews.

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:02.467
There is even a play that's called Photograph 51

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:07.100
that really describes the fascinating life of Rosalind Franklin.

00:12:07.133 --> 00:12:14.133
And I might as well mention that already here she worked on DNA for 27 months,

00:12:14.167 --> 00:12:17.800
and then she left and started to study viruses.

00:12:17.833 --> 00:12:23.400
Did some major contributions to study of virus structure. She really pioneered that work.

00:12:23.433 --> 00:12:29.433
And then in 1956, she was diagnosed with ovarian cancer,

00:12:29.467 --> 00:12:33.333
and she died in 1958.  She was never nominated for a Nobel Prize.

00:12:33.367 --> 00:12:40.367
But she was clearly a person who could have been a very strong candidate for that.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:46.100
So this photograph, it went well, but the third thing that was very, very crucial,

00:12:46.133 --> 00:12:53.367
was that in order to make the model building... to build the models,

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:57.367
Watson had to cut out the different nucleotides,

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:59.967
and he used Davidson's textbook, which everyone was using.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.900
That was the chemistry of its time.

00:13:02.933 --> 00:13:07.500
And then there was another person, again in the same room

00:13:07.533 --> 00:13:09.767
where they were at Cavendish laboratories, Jerry Donohue,

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:12.233
who was from California, like people called him.

00:13:12.267 --> 00:13:16.167
And Jerry Donohue for some reason said that I think you shouldn't use the structure

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:18.967
that is there in Davidson's textbook.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.500
You should use the other form of bases,

00:13:20.533 --> 00:13:26.333
and I can just say Watson said, oh what are the two form of bases? I mean, he didn't know much of chemistry.

00:13:26.367 --> 00:13:33.833
But he decided not to use the enol form, but upon the advice of Jerry Donohue he used the keto form.

00:13:33.867 --> 00:13:37.100
And that was what really solved the whole thing

00:13:37.133 --> 00:13:40.467
because then one could build the final model.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:42.300
And that is when it all converged.

00:13:42.333 --> 00:13:49.033
And the final model was the really, let's say the identification of the structure

00:13:49.067 --> 00:13:52.567
was in the morning of February 28, 1953.

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:57.900
And here's a few days later with Watson and Crick in a very famous picture

00:13:57.933 --> 00:13:59.933
illustrating what the model looks like.

00:13:59.967 --> 00:14:02.333
Now once one had this model,

00:14:02.367 --> 00:14:09.200
everything became very clear because here was a beautiful simple molecule

00:14:09.233 --> 00:14:15.600
that when it replicated, retained the same structure in its semi-conservative replication.

00:14:15.633 --> 00:14:22.233
It also had these bases that could come in any order,

00:14:22.267 --> 00:14:28.033
and therefore could represent an information carrying device.

00:14:28.067 --> 00:14:34.700
And of course, very soon after this one started to study what could the genetic language be.

00:14:34.733 --> 00:14:40.733
And since there are four bases and there are twenty amino acids,

00:14:40.767 --> 00:14:44.700
clearly it was not enough to have just pairs of bases,

00:14:44.733 --> 00:14:47.067
because that would not suffice for all the amino acids,

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.100
so there must be triplets and then

00:14:49.133 --> 00:14:53.567
progressively one could interpret the language

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:58.067
that nature has used ever since life started to develop...

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:05.600
this DNA designed life that started to develop, and this was some 3.8 billion years ago.

00:15:05.633 --> 00:15:10.133
So the impact was absolutely enormous,

00:15:10.167 --> 00:15:13.467
and the story goes of course that on that February 28th,

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:18.867
Watson and Crick went down to the pub that they used to visit after their day's work,

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:21.733
and said that we have found the molecule of life.

00:15:21.767 --> 00:15:25.767
Probably it's a good story. No one has managed to substantiate that.

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:30.433
And one, for example, one has interviewed Odile Crick, while she was still alive

00:15:30.467 --> 00:15:34.967
and asked her was something special on that February 28?

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:38.067
And she said, well, normally when Crick came home

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:41.300
there was always some wild story about the great discovery that he had made

00:15:41.333 --> 00:15:45.200
because he was always a hypomaniac person,

00:15:45.233 --> 00:15:48.067
but on this day he had some very good reasons to be that.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:52.300
So here's Watson receiving the prize in 1962,

00:15:52.333 --> 00:15:55.100
from the hands of His Majesty the King.

00:15:55.133 --> 00:16:03.967
And he was the third youngest Nobel laureate in Physiology or Medicine,

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:07.167
the youngest being Frederick Banting,

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:11.767
and then Josh Lederberg were also younger than Watson.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:16.567
32 and 33 years each. But Watson was 34 years old.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:19.767
And had the privilege of a long, rich life

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:25.033
in which he could come back and recollect and discuss that February.

00:16:25.067 --> 00:16:34.067
But before that perhaps, let me illustrate that I have tried to introduce in different chapters

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:42.067
in this book that I have just recently published some small haikus.

00:16:42.100 --> 00:16:44.467
And the haiku that related to the chapter that discusses DNA simply reads,

00:16:44.500 --> 00:16:46.333
The double helix

00:16:46.367 --> 00:16:48.133
Eternity in a string

00:16:48.167 --> 00:16:49.633
Symmetry well used

00:16:49.667 --> 00:16:53.367
And I want to emphasize the eternity

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:56.333
because this is the language that has been used ever since

00:16:56.367 --> 00:16:59.667
the genetic language was invented

00:16:59.700 --> 00:17:03.533
or when life originated in the world.

